#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186102023 :tutorial/content "To divide powers of the same base, we simply *subtract* the exponents. This is known as the **quotient rule for exponents**. For instance, in the expression\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}3}^5 \\div {\\color{blue}3}^2\n$$\n\nboth powers have the same base $({\\color{blue}3})$. Therefore, we can simplify this expression by subtracting the exponents:\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}3}^5 \\div {\\color{blue}3}^2 = {\\color{blue}3}^{5-2} = {\\color{blue}3}^3\n$$\n\n**Note:** The quotient rule for exponents is really just a quicker alternative to the following process:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3^{5} \\div 3^{2} &=  \\\\\n3^{3 + 2} \\div 3^{2} &=  \\\\\n3^{3} \\cdot 3^{2} \\div 3^{2}_(⏟)_(= 1) &=  \\\\\n3^{3} \\cdot 1 &=  \\\\\n&= 3^{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "4547d33f-6904-539f-b917-599af56aac3b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8158 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186102024 :tutorial/content "We can use the quotient rule for exponents with negative exponents too. For example, to simplify $3^{-5}\\div 3^{-3}$, we subtract the exponents:\n\n$$\n3^{-5} \\div 3^{-3} = 3^{-5-(-3)} = 3^{-2}\n$$\n\nNow, to evaluate $3^{-2}$, we find the reciprocal of the base $(3)$ and then raise that reciprocal to the same power but with the opposite sign:\n\n$$\n3^{-2} = \\left(\\dfrac{1}{3}\\right)^2 =\\dfrac{1}{3} \\times \\dfrac{1}{3} = \\dfrac{1}{9}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "b127450f-67ae-5232-a082-2242758636fd" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8160 :tutorial/title "The Quotient Rule With Negative Exponents"}]}
